  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nWV imagery showing main upper low now over western Nevada. Low \nwill continue lifting out northward as a secondary low eventually \ncloses off over SoCal and Arizona. Leading trough from the low \nextends N-S along the AZ/NM state line with good drying behind it.\nThis trough delineates the western edge of this evening's \nconvection. A weakening surface front from western Arizona down \nacross the Baja will continue east possibly near the AZ/NM before \nwashing out. The upper trough will continue to slowly drifting \neast over central New Mexico Friday afternoon before washing out. \nThis will usher drier air into the western CWA (PWs of around .6 \ninches compared to 1.0 inch in the east). Decent CAPE values will \ncontinue Friday, along with some speed shear. Thus expect \nscattered convection again from about Deming east, with more \nisolated storms to the west, though some severe storms still \npossible either side of this line. \n\nSaturday and Sunday...upper low continues to slowly lift \nnorthward from Arizona up to southern Utah. This will push deeper \nlayered moisture east of the RG Valley, where decent chances of \nstorms will continue. Expect best storm coverage from the RG \nValley, with just isolated showers/storms to the west. Lowering \nPWs all areas suggest less chance of heavy rain/flooding. Some \nsevere storm potential will continue but should be limited mostly \nto the RG Valley east. \n\nMonday and Tuesday...main upper low exits into the central Plains\nwith drier air aloft (at least at upper levels). GFS shows mid-\nlevel winds (700mb) remaining southerly and keeping some shallower\nmoisture in the eastern CWA, with a slight chance of showers \nMonday from El Paso east. Moisture clears out Tuesday as mid level\nwinds briefly turn more southwest. \n\nWednesday and Thursday...GFS remains bullish in bringing up more \nmoisture with the help of a Pacific short wave approaching the \narea. Thus GFS has west two days here while the ECMWF remains \nmostly dry.",
